<h1>George Cardenas</h1>
<p>Cook County Board of Review commissioner, former alderman. Candidate for mayor of Chicago, on the ballot February 23, 2027.</p>
<p>On the Board of Review since 2022 after twenty years as 12th Ward alderman; 62, born in Durango, Mexico, a Navy veteran and former auditor who lives in McKinley Park. Filed 31,500 signatures within an hour of the deadline.</p>
<p><a href="/directdemocracy/app/candidate/wPkdvjAnedapNl1KNSw1YANnKEB2">Full platform, open to comments</a></p>
<p><a href="https://cardenas4chicago.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign website (cardenas4chicago.com)</a></p>
<h2>What sets them apart</h2>
<p>Only Cardenas: $3,000 a year to 150,000 working families, a $250 million program, plus universal pre-K.</p>
<p>Vacant property: After years of documented abandonment, the city could take long-empty lots and buildings by eminent domain, with payment, and put them back to use.</p>
<p>Pensions: Shared sacrifice from the city and its workers, with current retirees protected. Quigley agrees; Giannoulias and Mendoza would restructure and pay ahead.</p>
<p>City Hall: A new auditor general for waste and contracts, and $5 billion in bonds to start rebuilding water mains, roads, and transit.</p>
<h2>Where they split from the field</h2>
<p>Pensions: Workers share the cost, with Quigley. Restructure the funds and pay ahead: Giannoulias and Mendoza. Change the state constitution: Wilson. Bankruptcy on the table: Kitover.</p>
<p>Money for households: $3,000 a year to working families. A guaranteed income: Ma. A $22 minimum wage: Giannoulias.</p>
<h2>In their words</h2>
<ul>
<li>Send $3,000 a year to 150,000 working families, and expand pre-K to every child. <q>$3,000 annual stipends for qualified working families</q> (<a href="https://www.cardenas4chicago.com/pillar-parenting-matters.html" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign platform (cardenas4chicago.com)</a>)</li>
<li>After years of documented abandonment, take long-empty lots and buildings by eminent domain, with payment, and put them back to use. <q>It targets chronic abandonment that imposes costs on surrounding families.</q> (<a href="https://www.cardenas4chicago.com/pillar-economic-growth.html" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign platform (cardenas4chicago.com)</a>)</li>
<li>Fix pensions through shared sacrifice from the city and its workers, with current retirees&#39; benefits protected. <q>Shared sacrifice from city and workers</q> (<a href="https://www.cardenas4chicago.com/pillar-pensions.html" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign platform (cardenas4chicago.com)</a>)</li>
<li>Create an auditor general to track waste, contracts, and unfinished audit findings. <q>When city government is disorganized, families pay the price, and when fiscal controls are weak, neighborhoods will suffer,</q> (<a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/former-ald-george-cardenas-to-run-for-mayor/" rel="nofollow noopener">CBS Chicago, June 16, 2026</a>)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Campaign money</h2>
<ul>
<li>Committee: Cardenas4Chicago and Friends of George A. Cardenas</li>
<li>Raised: $174K</li>
<li>Spent: $149K</li>
<li>Cash on hand: $208K</li>
<li>Raised July 1 to Sept. 30, 2026; spent Jan. 1 to Sept. 30, 2026, both committees combined. His older committee from the City Council did most of the year&#39;s spending. <a href="https://elections.il.gov/CampaignDisclosure/CommitteeDetail.aspx?id=41169" rel="nofollow noopener">Source</a></li>
</ul>
<p>Money figures are each committee&#39;s quarterly reports to the Illinois State Board of Elections: spent is total expenditures from January 1 to September 30, 2026 (or since the committee formed), cash is funds available on September 30.</p>
<h2>Support</h2>
<p>0.5% of likely voters in the ChicagoSpeaks poll, Sept. 2026 (Mansueto Institute / NORC). 3.3 points of support per $1 million spent.</p>
<h2>On money</h2>
<p>Would create an auditor general to chase waste and contract overruns, reserve a fifth of city purchasing for small businesses, and send $3,000 a year to 150,000 working families.</p>
<ul>
<li><q>Put the numbers on the table, control spending and rebuild confidence in city finances.</q> (<a href="https://cardenas4chicago.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">cardenas4chicago.com</a>)</li>
<li><q>$3,000 annual stipends for qualified working families</q> (<a href="https://www.cardenas4chicago.com/pillar-parenting-matters.html" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign platform (cardenas4chicago.com)</a>)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Pizza</h2>
<p>No position on record.</p>
<h2>Also on the record</h2>
<ul>
<li>A four-year Navy veteran and Lane Tech graduate who came to Chicago from Durango at 13. (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Cardenas" rel="nofollow noopener">en.wikipedia.org</a>)</li>
</ul>
